Quoting  Matt Weiner  <matt@mattweiner.net>:
>Didn't Gary Gygax have the opposite view of D&D; that it was about
>fighting and treasure rather than character development? Certatinly
>it's playable either way.

xD&D, especially early D&Ds, is an interesting case. Of course when Gygax
first did what was effectively an insane Chainmail variant, no-one really
knew what a roleplaying game was going to be - it's not surprising that it
ended up as an odd mix of skirmish wargaming and roleplaying, and later
xD&Ds have continued with that mix - hence the game is pretty playable
either as "straight" skirmish wargaming or "straight" RPG.

So... I think initially, yes, Gygax did think more of fighting and
treasure, and the roleplaying elements emerged more as people _did_
with the game things that hadn't been done with games before.
